13. Where Axcelis fits

Axcelis Technologies, ticker ACLS, is one of the most focused public-market ways to get exposure to ion implantation.

Its core business is ion implant equipment.

Its Purion platform spans high-current, medium-current and high-energy applications across advanced logic, memory, image sensors, mature-node devices and power semiconductors.

In February 2026, Axcelis introduced the Purion H6, a next-generation high-current implanter aimed at logic, advanced memory, image sensors and mature technology applications.

Axcelis emphasized dose control, particle control, source stability and throughput.

That list tells you what customers actually buy:

not "ions," but repeatable manufacturing economics.

14. Where Applied Materials fits

Applied Materials, ticker AMAT, is much broader.

It sells equipment across deposition, materials engineering, patterning-related processes and other steps inside the fab.

Applied also owns the Varian ion implantation business, which it acquired in 2011.

That gives Applied exposure to ion implantation without making implant the sole thesis.

This creates an interesting contrast:

Axcelis = concentrated implant specialist.

Applied Materials = diversified wafer-fab equipment platform with implant as one piece.

Neither framing automatically makes one the better investment.

But the business-model difference matters.

A focused supplier can have more upside to a favorable product cycle and more exposure to a narrow technology category.

A diversified supplier can benefit from many process inflections while being less dependent on one tool family.

15. Why power semiconductors matter for implant

Ion implantation is not only about leading-edge AI logic.

Axcelis has built meaningful exposure to power semiconductors, including silicon carbide, or SiC.

Power devices manage and convert electricity.

They are important in electric vehicles, industrial systems, energy infrastructure and increasingly power-hungry computing environments.

Silicon carbide is especially useful for high-voltage and high-temperature applications.

But SiC is difficult to implant because its crystal is hard and dopant activation can require demanding processing.

That creates specialized equipment needs.

Axcelis has developed dedicated Purion Power products for Si and SiC power devices.

This is an important lesson for the course:

A semiconductor equipment company's AI exposure can be indirect.

AI data centers drive electricity demand, but the same vendor may also serve EVs, industrial power and other markets.

You have to separate the technical bottleneck from the end-market mix.

16. Why memory matters

DRAM and NAND also require ion implantation.

The exact recipes differ from logic.

Memory manufacturers need controlled doping for transistor and peripheral circuitry, and 3D structures can make dose, angle and depth control increasingly difficult.

Axcelis explicitly targets advanced memory with its implant platforms.

For investors, memory exposure introduces cyclicality.

If DRAM or NAND manufacturers cut capex, equipment orders can decline even if the long-term technical need for implantation remains.

So again separate:

Structural process importance

from

Near-term customer spending.

17. Implant versus deposition and etch

Ion implantation is strategically important, but its economic structure differs from some other process steps.

Deposition and etch intensity can rise sharply as chips become more three-dimensional and layer counts increase.

Ion implant remains critical, but the number of implant steps and tool content do not necessarily scale in exactly the same way.

That means you should not assume every semiconductor-equipment category benefits equally from AI-driven complexity.

The right question is:

Does the architecture change increase the number, difficulty, value or bottleneck status of this process step?

For implant, much of the value comes from precision, dose control, depth control, contamination management and specialized device requirements.

